          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best smoker for beginners?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A pellet grill like the Traeger Pro 575 is the easiest way to start, because the controller holds temperature for you. If you want charcoal flavor and do not mind a little vent work, the Weber Smokey Mountain is the classic beginner smoker and teaches good fire management.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a pellet smoker or a charcoal smoker better?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Neither is better outright. Pellet smokers are easier and more consistent but produce a milder smoke flavor. Charcoal smokers give stronger flavor and better bark for less money but need more attention. Choose based on how much time you want to spend tending the fire.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does a smoker last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on build and care. Heavy steel and ceramic smokers can last many years, while thin-steel offsets and budget pellet grills often rust or need parts sooner. Emptying ash, covering the smoker and storing it dry extends the life of any model considerably.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you use a smoker in the rain?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Charcoal and offset smokers can be used in light rain, but they burn more fuel to hold temperature. Pellet and gravity-fed smokers have electronics that must stay dry, so use a cover designed for operation or a sheltered spot that is still fully open to the air.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size smoker do I need for a family of four?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Around 400 to 600 square inches of main grate is plenty for a typical family cook, such as a pork butt and a rack of ribs. Check that a full brisket fits the grate's length if you plan to cook one.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a water pan in my smoker?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Water pans help stabilize temperature and add humidity, which is why bullet smokers like the Weber Smokey Mountain use one. Many pellet and offset cooks skip it. It is a useful tool on charcoal smokers, especially for beginners learning to hold steady heat.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is an electric smoker as good as a pellet smoker?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Electric smokers are simpler and cheaper but produce less smoke flavor and no real bark at low temperatures. Pellet smokers burn wood and give more flavor. See our best electric smoker guide if simplicity and price are your priorities.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
